  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nThe forecast is generally on track for the next seven days with a\nbrief reprieve from the heat early in the next week followed by a\nreturn to hotter and mostly rain-free conditions. In the near\nterm, per SPC and the NCAR AI/ML suite, there is a highly \nconditional threat for getting a few windy storms in what has \nturned out to be an unusually well-mixed airmass today. The \nboundary layer conditions are not supportive of storm development \nin our area, so it will take a good bit of forcing from the mid \nlevel trough and associated cold front currently digging southward\ninto the area if we are to see any late this afternoon and \nevening. If storms do indeed develop this far west (i.e., east \ncentral MS), seasonably strong low to mid level westerlies and \nsteeper mid level lapse rates could support a damaging wind \nthreat, particularly if any multicell clusters can get going. \nOtherwise, most locations over the forecast will experience quiet \nconditions as we go through the near term. /EC/\n\nSlightly drier air will advect into the area in the wake of the \nfront and hang around the region Sunday into Monday. Highs will \ngenerally range from the upper 80s to mid 90s each day, with dew \npoints from the upper 60s to lower 70s. Unfortunately, this'll all\nbe short-lived as we move into the mid and latter portions of the\nweek. Strong high pressure aloft will again re- assert itself \nacross the Lower Mississippi River Valley during this time. High \ntemperatures will again begin climbing back into the mid and upper\n90s areawide. Moisture levels will also begin creeping up, \npossibly again leading to another bout of dangerous heat stress as\nwe end the forecast period. Rain chances also will be tough to \ncome by during this time, with only an isolated shower or storm \npossible. /19/EC/",
